#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500454} :activity/id #uuid "85aae590-c9ef-573e-b23e-5f7bc75274d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050913 :topic/id #uuid "70cae1cb-dd81-5ef6-8e36-14c86064b3f2" :topic/math-academy-id 3628} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500400 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31cb6b88-21af-58b2-9bbc-36b86d29f9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27x-9y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241619} {:answer/id #uuid "616d62d4-fd1d-56e0-aea1-01be6ea44e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242058} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c02940-6aae-590b-a846-f3b0124feef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x-2y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242587} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fd5bee-c66f-5b57-832d-ffd1bffd7ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242869} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3e0d5a-55f1-5492-8d8f-c4889137f0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27x-9y-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243137}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243137} :answer-field/id #uuid "b05d6b4a-98d6-5cb1-8fde-f65da21468d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5a92b889-cf95-57c9-b2d1-07f0f25b0f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-108857" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{9x-3y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=y+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3(3x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{9z-2}{3z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\int (1+\\frac{2}{9z-2})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2|) & =x+K \\\\ z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2| & =3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =3x+C \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=27x-9y-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "40ce0eca-d941-5f5c-a7fa-03f69121d55c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500419}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500419 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1cd5c9-94cf-5052-88d3-2d79e7000e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(x+y)^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499676} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7d3c01-c243-59f4-a934-9a1f2a84dfec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+y)^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499754} {:answer/id #uuid "91e8717a-479f-5d6c-acf1-f66f4d598536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499937} {:answer/id #uuid "a2447c41-aa34-5dd8-a83b-c22d7a64de82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(x+y)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499970} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ac85a2-5b20-585b-9916-beffaa56d4e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499754} :answer-field/id #uuid "73573c33-d66a-5018-8523-c62476af953e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4eeaa0f9-0c45-52de-a336-6cd7b380095c" :question/math-academy-id "q-149753" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2-\\sqrt{9x+9y}}{3\\sqrt{x+y}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-3\\sqrt{x+y}}{3\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{2-3\\sqrt{z}}{3\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{z}}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\int \\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ {z}^{3/2} & =x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^{3/2}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=(x+y)^{3/2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "82e6922c-62a9-566b-8248-260400cee30c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac2be57-88bd-5991-89e7-f183e3afe98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499677} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3d2de3-50fc-59bc-b9c5-3dc40d87957c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499683} {:answer/id #uuid "9e590015-bbfb-5679-9c40-c0209b1e3c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20z-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499961} {:answer/id #uuid "a98675ac-9d60-5df5-b761-a3ed53134265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-10z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499986} {:answer/id #uuid "d9618560-52b1-56c2-8d53-9263e74d26fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15-10z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499961} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e7fc7cb-acd9-557f-b874-53ca29199374" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c8d6de1e-2ae5-5e90-a986-dc528762dc4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108742" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=20y-12x.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=5y-3x,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4(5y-3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=5y-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3) & =4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=20z-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=20z-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "88bd2953-dbda-5396-b020-f9063b5433d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500400}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a6bddc-ab82-5ec6-afde-d00b334c9e79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241264} {:answer/id #uuid "09f70782-96ea-53f6-8f9e-80c4729c6bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241268} {:answer/id #uuid "d579b6f2-cdce-5402-83f9-c79bb237818f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{z}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243069} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c9abf5-c7a7-5718-a3b6-86da7dc6fb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{z}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243119} {:answer/id #uuid "e537cb6a-f6c7-570c-adef-a9027acdab1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243217}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243217} :answer-field/id #uuid "10ce90bd-39f0-5d28-917d-33cd6143f545" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10d103bc-2f56-5346-9216-0cbfc3385289" :question/math-academy-id "q-108736" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}+6xy+3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3(x+y)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3{z}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3{z}^{2}+1.$"} :step/id #uuid "b1d95414-932c-5d71-9e94-9b53243552e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500420}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b96fb1-181d-54b4-95a2-bf57eb2d175c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3z}{2z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499705} {:answer/id #uuid "1d44a2c0-09f5-5967-91ed-90c7342a9d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+z}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499717} {:answer/id #uuid "a28063b0-4299-5858-a67b-6b6976a982bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2z}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499972} {:answer/id #uuid "b5cc3aae-4e04-5ba4-b4f4-e869f79cc091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+3z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500024} {:answer/id #uuid "d0386823-3ef9-54e9-9c8e-ab1f46c69a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+z}{2z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499972} :answer-field/id #uuid "f47fb17e-8686-5981-8687-3cc41d6ed00d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "489ad320-9026-5fb6-bac4-8d732b399bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-107802" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1-3x-3y}{x+y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-3(x+y)}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{1-3z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1-2z}{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{1-2z}{z}.$"} :step/id #uuid "f3072577-7453-50a8-b7a6-6b0c5b209a24" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500438}}] :activity/title "Further Solving First-Order ODEs by Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499650}]}
